  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NESA Center Executive Seminar 03-20</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This course seeks to stimulate dialogue on new threats to the countries in the NESA region, and beyond. National security strategies are now addressing issues such as cyber-attacks on military and civilian infrastructures, the challenges of e-government security, managing developments in artificial intelligence, and even the use of space as a theater of security. The reality is that there is often little cooperation across borders among the countries in the NESA AOR, or even globally, to address these issues. How can states better incorporate technology into the security domain that might sometimes exceed their very capacity to do so? What, besides increased trust among partner or neighboring states is necessary to address these challenges? This seminar will look at both technological and policy implications of such cooperative measures. Ideally, participants will have a working knowledge of the use of technology in security within their country.</narrative>
  </description>
